// `copy`, `give` and `truncate` name a BINDING — a local, a parameter, a field, a member, a pattern — exactly as `type`,
// `slot` and `file` do, and like them they name no type. The message names the word and the rule.
type value give { public int32 x; }
fn int32 main() { return 0; }
// ⚠️ Not in tree-sitter-kama/test/parse-errors.txt: tree-sitter accepts a reserved word at a DECLARATION site, for every
// reserved word alike — KR-29, which is the row that owns it. The editor shows this file clean; the compiler does not.
